#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063912 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e05385-7cb6-5f21-b0d8-a7641d912d5b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function"}] :question/id #uuid "121d5f8c-3b38-5c75-aec1-646dc7d2f2f8" :question/math-academy-id "e-14719" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{2-3x}{4+5x}$ and the point $P\\left(-1,-5\\right)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ determine the slope of $y=f^{-1}(x)$ at the point $\\left(-5,-1\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(-1, -5).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2-3x}{4+5x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{22}{(4+5x)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =-22\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(-1, -5)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(-5, -1)$ lies on $y=f^{-1}(x).$ \n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(-5) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(-5))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-22)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{22}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063913 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4270c31c-80f1-5b5d-bca8-e16a47206c2b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function From a Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "d113528f-a3ff-5f28-b071-bcf355a36b45" :question/math-academy-id "e-14720" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/assets/8fb287885c948b7e563b5fa878dd204dacbedea311490c4573aa883bff992662.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ and its inverse $y=f^{-1}(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(2, 0)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$\nThe point $B$ has coordinates $(0,2)$ and lies on $y=f^{-1}(x).$ If $f'(2)=-\\dfrac{3}{2},$ what is $(f^{-1})'(0)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B \\,(0,2)$ lies on $f^{-1},$ we have $f^{-1}(0) = 2.$ We're also told that $f'(2)=-\\dfrac{3}{2}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063914 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b492068c-2bd2-5611-baab-780959d24c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function Using a Table"}] :question/id #uuid "9e531872-846f-5e75-97c0-dd2db3515558" :question/math-academy-id "e-14721" :question/problem "The following table gives the values of $f(x), f'(x),g(x),$ and $g'(x)$ at some points. Find $(f^{-1}\\cdot g)'(1)$ of the product of functions.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | $f'(x)$ | $g(x)$ | $g'(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $-3$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $3$ | $5$ |" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse $f^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(f^{-1})'(x) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function $f^{-1}(x)g(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(f^{-1}\\cdot g)'(x)=\\dfrac{g(x)}{f'(f^{-1}(x))}+g'(x)f^{-1}(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n(f^{-1}\\cdot g)'(1) =\\dfrac{g(1)}{f'(f^{-1}(1))}+g'(1)f^{-1}(1).\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain $f^{-1}(1)=2,$ $g(1)=-3,$ $g'(1)=1,$ and $f'(2)=-1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(1) & =\\frac{g(1)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))}+{g}^{′}(1){f}^{-1}(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{{f}^{′}(2)}+1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{-1}+2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063915 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2a80aa-7882-50eb-bfe0-c0eeab72161d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Derivative Using Properties of Inverse Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "d38ac709-38e8-51a1-8e08-14913c77639c" :question/math-academy-id "e-14722" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x) =3e^{x}-1.$ Given that $y = f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $(f^{-1})'(2).$" :question/worked-solution "To determine $(f^{-1})'(2),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\left(f^{-1}\\right)'(2) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(2))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of $f^{-1}(2).$ To do this, we solve the equation $f^{-1}(2) = x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(2) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =2 \\\\ 3{e}^{x}-1 & =2 \\\\ 3{e}^{x} & =3 \\\\ {e}^{x} & =1 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f^{-1}(2) = 0.$\n\nNow, we calculate $f'(0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =3{e}^{x}-1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =3{e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3{e}^{0} \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279528 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4270c31c-80f1-5b5d-bca8-e16a47206c2b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function From a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9434b4b6-d22d-5fba-9463-bb96e89da88f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279530} {:answer/id #uuid "44816eb8-d92e-5595-b392-294c54d4f51f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279531} {:answer/id #uuid "4af93f4c-51d3-5be4-99b1-69e41cd80363" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279532} {:answer/id #uuid "5c808965-c925-5457-9cd7-80b6af9c3fb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279533} {:answer/id #uuid "3c257aa8-4e12-55fc-aaa6-2dd57ab595ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279534}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c257aa8-4e12-55fc-aaa6-2dd57ab595ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279534} :answer-field/id #uuid "3afc419b-7ff7-529b-bd3f-9a0d8e0fef51" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279529}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "679fd2b3-0e7d-591f-b40a-c8583c6e0a69" :question/math-academy-id "q-110127" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/assets/e09f5a90c683876f849577cd75af3c77cc21c6f9af10c5a4ece47718aa33f738.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a piecewise linear function $y=f(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(2,4)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $(4,2)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(4)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $(4,2)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(4)=2.$\nThe slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ for $x\\in [0,4]$ is $-\\frac{1}{2}$, hence, ${f}^{′}(2)=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279535 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4270c31c-80f1-5b5d-bca8-e16a47206c2b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function From a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a09bbb6b-847b-59b6-99a1-f194c396a855" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279537} {:answer/id #uuid "2d973d9f-7362-51c6-b8bf-1955272edfe3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279538} {:answer/id #uuid "a9168eb8-94d2-5d8c-bdef-8759c2bfb850" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279539} {:answer/id #uuid "481f5132-0309-5f94-b274-f3716fefb545" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279540} {:answer/id #uuid "1aa292b8-4ad7-5bbd-8258-0c90f60206b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279541}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d973d9f-7362-51c6-b8bf-1955272edfe3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279538} :answer-field/id #uuid "27e44538-9754-50ca-9436-c89b95106aaa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279536}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "30b07781-82ca-5995-998f-b7f04d3afb4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-110129" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/assets/265866e56e07a86dd4d6f43ce5d752d62491dea962c3dce3273b924e6e2b362b.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a piecewise linear function $y=f(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(2,3)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $(3,2)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(3)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $(3,2)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(3)=2.$ \nThe slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ for $x\\in [0,2.5]$ is $2$, hence, ${f}^{′}(2)=2.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is \n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(3) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(3))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279542 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4270c31c-80f1-5b5d-bca8-e16a47206c2b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function From a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc9bf329-1eed-55c7-a672-f78fbd363769" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279544} {:answer/id #uuid "014896cb-38e4-55f7-835b-62f9fbf455dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279545} {:answer/id #uuid "7fd6599b-4203-5576-ab15-1332d294ab7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279546} {:answer/id #uuid "b55bcc0f-a090-5ff4-929c-931a4f7d99c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279547} {:answer/id #uuid "f9a28e06-914c-5135-870a-9a7021354620" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279548}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "014896cb-38e4-55f7-835b-62f9fbf455dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279545} :answer-field/id #uuid "068b4da3-a2e0-5ae7-a4bd-7cb5bd0c479f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279543}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "897b7921-24ed-546d-9505-bfabbd58980a" :question/math-academy-id "q-110198" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/assets/ba762e13c4015b43b99f9038ee63a247584345cd535e12b80c1f3b0d9e50e70b.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a piecewise linear function $y=f(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(1,0)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $(0,1)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $(0,1)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(0)=1.$ \nThe slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ for $x\\in [0,2]$ is $-2$, hence, ${f}^{′}(1)=-2.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is \n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279549 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b492068c-2bd2-5611-baab-780959d24c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function Using a Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8cc2dbe6-2683-5c50-9b19-63fd9174cf06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{31}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279551} {:answer/id #uuid "242b6e9e-1054-54f8-884f-faec3ec6194c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279552} {:answer/id #uuid "eca4998f-0622-5cfb-8cc2-b8c825273700" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{46}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279553} {:answer/id #uuid "75dbe5e6-9df1-5920-a28a-bcb0687ff554" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279554} {:answer/id #uuid "88412c1d-bd9b-5346-aa51-252c52f9ee28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{28}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279555}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8cc2dbe6-2683-5c50-9b19-63fd9174cf06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{31}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279551} :answer-field/id #uuid "a46c9094-ba55-5f98-a3f1-1d3c3bedbc27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279550}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "75bdddd1-2f9e-5cb2-9924-d4847b6838c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-110272" :question/problem "The following table gives the values of $f(x),{f}^{′}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points. Find $({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(4)$ of the product of functions.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $6$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $5$ |\n| $3$ | $4$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $-3$ |" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function ${f}^{-1}(x)g(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(x)=\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}+{g}^{′}(x){f}^{-1}(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(4)=\\frac{g(4)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))}+{g}^{′}(4){f}^{-1}(4).\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(4)=3,$ $g(4)=-1,$ ${g}^{′}(4)=5,$ and ${f}^{′}(3)=-2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(4) & =\\frac{g(4)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))}+{g}^{′}(4){f}^{-1}(4) \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{{f}^{′}(3)}+5\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{-2}+15 \\\\  & =\\frac{31}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279556 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2a80aa-7882-50eb-bfe0-c0eeab72161d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Derivative Using Properties of Inverse Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58514938-c1e9-5d63-8917-72463ffc6022" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279558} {:answer/id #uuid "1454d265-817d-5f47-9c10-7639b426a74d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279559} {:answer/id #uuid "a39e44b5-0bd0-593a-8fa1-67c1db7249ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279560} {:answer/id #uuid "bae59a1b-3263-561a-9cd4-4449ef437b31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279561} {:answer/id #uuid "a2aaf225-8d73-5350-a42a-e69aa296da51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279562}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bae59a1b-3263-561a-9cd4-4449ef437b31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279561} :answer-field/id #uuid "126cafa6-7156-51f1-b07b-bb36ae50f873" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279557}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d198f76c-2fe3-50e7-9351-8eafd2f01101" :question/math-academy-id "q-110341" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+5x+2.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2).$" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(2).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(2)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(2) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =2 \\\\ {x}^{3}+5x+2 & =2 \\\\ x({x}^{2}+5) & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(2)=0.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{3}+5x+2 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}+5 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3\\cdot 0+5 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279563 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e05385-7cb6-5f21-b0d8-a7641d912d5b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2214a2ef-dfcd-59d9-954f-6871162ceb57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279565} {:answer/id #uuid "5a6f4abc-bea0-51c9-8e2d-5e031ab0c129" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279566} {:answer/id #uuid "3df97db9-3edc-5e56-9da4-33f6081aac5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279567} {:answer/id #uuid "08cc8d48-106c-514d-9cb5-2fa415f7034d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279568} {:answer/id #uuid "0d8aceb1-18dd-5a1d-9de1-cc181dfa7657" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279569}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2214a2ef-dfcd-59d9-954f-6871162ceb57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279565} :answer-field/id #uuid "65c78d50-f7fb-52d7-96cb-16cbbe422d3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279564}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9defc19b-b540-5a93-90de-15be5571aa37" :question/math-academy-id "q-15651" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{x-3}}$ and the point $P(7,\\frac{3}{4})$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ determine the slope of $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ at the point $(\\frac{3}{4},7).$" :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(7,\\frac{3}{4}).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{x-3}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{3}{4(x-3)^{3/2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(7) & =-\\frac{3}{32}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(7,\\frac{3}{4})$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(\\frac{3}{4},7)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(\\frac{3}{4}) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(\\frac{3}{4}))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(7)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{3}{32})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{32}{3}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279570 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e05385-7cb6-5f21-b0d8-a7641d912d5b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66c87dd3-1c92-52cb-92c3-5a09eb773c49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279572} {:answer/id #uuid "dc1915c9-6c2b-55c3-b5b0-5df88ed581d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279573} {:answer/id #uuid "2d5bae6b-20ac-538e-891d-8254734ca436" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279574} {:answer/id #uuid "a8edbfcb-1b92-512b-aa38-0c2aafb2eb8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279575} {:answer/id #uuid "311e8ecc-4b5d-58be-b482-3bbae10a1e7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279576}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "311e8ecc-4b5d-58be-b482-3bbae10a1e7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279576} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc98d4c2-2902-5abf-9024-1bb5676fba2f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279571}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "71816a61-baa5-5230-bd29-159dd6440cd5" :question/math-academy-id "q-15652" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{3+4x}{2-3x}$ and the point $P(1,-7)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ determine the slope of $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ at the point $(-7,1).$" :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(1,-7).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{3+4x}{2-3x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{17}{(2-3x)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =17\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(1,-7)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(-7,1)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(-7) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(-7))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{17}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279577 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b492068c-2bd2-5611-baab-780959d24c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function Using a Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ddcd8470-cb6e-52a8-baa6-16cfad6bf91c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279579}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ddcd8470-cb6e-52a8-baa6-16cfad6bf91c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279579} :answer-field/id #uuid "63e79ecb-5a2b-5edd-b93c-554db2be15a3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279578}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "74b2f2fb-bda2-5fab-bba0-63ecfa763a2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-312584" :question/problem "Find ${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)$ of the product of functions, given the following table of values of $f(x),{f}^{′}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $-4$ | $2$ | $4$ | $-3$ |\n| $5$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $3$ | $0$ |\n\n${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function $g(x){f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(x)={g}^{′}(x){f}^{-1}(x)+\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(2)={g}^{′}(2){f}^{-1}(2)+\\frac{g(2)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(2)=5,$ $g(2)=4,$ ${g}^{′}(2)=-3,$ and ${f}^{′}(5)=-1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(2) & ={g}^{′}(2){f}^{-1}(2)+\\frac{g(2)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 5+\\frac{4}{{f}^{′}(5)} \\\\  & =-15+\\frac{4}{(-1)} \\\\  & =-15-4 \\\\  & =-19.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279580 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2a80aa-7882-50eb-bfe0-c0eeab72161d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Derivative Using Properties of Inverse Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b8e9a09-eb9e-504b-b42f-8c42ab38cdc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4\\ln(4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279582}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b8e9a09-eb9e-504b-b42f-8c42ab38cdc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4\\ln(4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279582} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0ac6665-8270-5b3f-aa0d-3ad34e91de23" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279581}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a7e4d2ce-43e6-5f2f-a26a-1291eab1840c" :question/math-academy-id "q-312588" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={4}^{x}+2.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(6).$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(6)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(6),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(6)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(6))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(6).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(6)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(6) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =6 \\\\ {4}^{x}+2 & =6 \\\\ {4}^{x} & =4 \\\\ {4}^{x} & ={4}^{1} \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(6)=1.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={4}^{x}+2 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={4}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & ={4}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(6) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(6))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279583 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2a80aa-7882-50eb-bfe0-c0eeab72161d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Derivative Using Properties of Inverse Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a454b79-7424-5cc5-9a21-f341ebc41a83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279585} {:answer/id #uuid "0cbece89-1b26-5184-9d69-8c70945c64c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279586}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0cbece89-1b26-5184-9d69-8c70945c64c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279586} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3a9d6f6-b957-5ea4-80e1-212c6531bdb9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279584}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c939b62d-8700-5d42-a705-6818a09c4515" :question/math-academy-id "q-312589" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2).$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0).$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(0).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(0)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(0) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe discard $x=0$ since the logarithm is not defined here. Therefore, ${f}^{-1}(0)=3.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(3),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2) \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x-2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =2\\cdot 3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-2)+\\frac{{3}^{2}}{3-2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279587 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4270c31c-80f1-5b5d-bca8-e16a47206c2b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function From a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "285d5e04-6dab-56cc-8d98-a8430a74c834" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279589} {:answer/id #uuid "d8d9e563-e33c-5037-b3ea-82ea794b6308" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279590}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8d9e563-e33c-5037-b3ea-82ea794b6308" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279590} :answer-field/id #uuid "19491414-5359-58d0-962c-8d3a1746c460" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279588}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7fd6ddaf-3c46-5030-b4a5-c0761f64013d" :question/math-academy-id "q-342561" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/assets/adcee8bddcbbd96e18d63f85f5b9ebf2edc52897cc1ddcca7e377194462f6bda.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ and its inverse $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(-1,0)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ The point $B$ has coordinates $(0,-1)$ and lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ If ${f}^{′}(-1)=3,$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)?$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $B\\,(0,-1)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(0)=-1.$\nWe're also told that ${f}^{′}(-1)=3.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279591 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4270c31c-80f1-5b5d-bca8-e16a47206c2b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function From a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67da14c0-cb28-53af-8e70-84ccda2d27fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279593}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67da14c0-cb28-53af-8e70-84ccda2d27fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279593} :answer-field/id #uuid "4cc73e86-36af-52ce-a446-19f7fb18768d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279592}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "337a9944-8b6d-5e9a-8280-a4fd810a5d34" :question/math-academy-id "q-342575" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/assets/ce948d3eade308a90ffa85a7c7a88a8c403a6620ffda7e6d24444d2abac0770c.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a piecewise linear function $y=f(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(1,1)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $(1,1)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1)?$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $(1,1)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(1)=1.$ \nThe slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ for $x\\in [-2,4]$ is $\\frac{2}{3}$, hence, ${f}^{′}(1)=\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is \n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279594 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e05385-7cb6-5f21-b0d8-a7641d912d5b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a0c5b6b-e340-5575-8040-e67e50780fa6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279596} {:answer/id #uuid "38f06589-d04c-547d-a8f1-0ce386c86214" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279597}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38f06589-d04c-547d-a8f1-0ce386c86214" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279597} :answer-field/id #uuid "32abd2ef-a001-5271-8715-9a083d0a399d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279595}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1feeaa24-053b-5b18-a92c-a34f2428102b" :question/math-academy-id "q-342579" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}+1}$ and the point $P(1,0)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0).$\n\n${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(1,0).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{(x-1)^{′}\\cdot ({x}^{2}+1)-(x-1)\\cdot {({x}^{2}+1)}^{′}}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1\\cdot ({x}^{2}+1)-(x-1)\\cdot 2x}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+1-2{x}^{2}+2x}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1+2x-{x}^{2}}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1+2(1)-{1}^{2}}{{({1}^{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(1,0)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(0,1)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279598 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e05385-7cb6-5f21-b0d8-a7641d912d5b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "709724cf-a5c4-5518-9d05-d0e382847be2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279600}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "709724cf-a5c4-5518-9d05-d0e382847be2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279600} :answer-field/id #uuid "e494bbd2-f727-5bb1-be2d-e7c49a134540" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279599}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2b4427d3-b344-5045-8e56-9bfadf272756" :question/math-academy-id "q-342580" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=3\\sqrt[3]{x}$ and the point $P(8,6)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(6).$\n\n${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(6)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(8,6).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =3\\sqrt[3]{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{x}^{-2/3} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(8) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{8}^{2}}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(8) & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(8,6)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(6,8)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(6) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(6))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(8)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279601 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e05385-7cb6-5f21-b0d8-a7641d912d5b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13dfb1f2-a005-5d13-ae89-04d337e5c46d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279603}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13dfb1f2-a005-5d13-ae89-04d337e5c46d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279603} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f44b7b1-53a5-502c-9ed2-d2363703b510" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279602}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0d9fadce-8516-5492-be9d-1ecb20a64fee" :question/math-academy-id "q-342585" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and the point $P(e,1)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1).$\n\n${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(e,1).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(e) & =\\frac{1}{e}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(e,1)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(1,e)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(e)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{e})} \\\\  & =e.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279604 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b492068c-2bd2-5611-baab-780959d24c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function Using a Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44ff3980-1fed-50f1-9128-e3ca6632b8ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279606} {:answer/id #uuid "bff14158-2799-57d3-9c92-9753e0e65652" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279607}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44ff3980-1fed-50f1-9128-e3ca6632b8ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279606} :answer-field/id #uuid "630d3a62-d1f0-5ad8-b11f-df4ca1ba102f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279605}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0b938f4e-7aed-514c-aa88-1f8816849a95" :question/math-academy-id "q-342596" :question/problem "Find ${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)$ of the product of functions, given the following table of values of $f(x),{f}^{′}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $0$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $3$ | $-1$ |\n\n${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function $g(x){f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(x)={g}^{′}(x){f}^{-1}(x)+\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(2)={g}^{′}(2){f}^{-1}(2)+\\frac{g(2)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(2)=1,$ $g(2)=-1,$ ${g}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{2},$ and ${f}^{′}(1)=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(2) & ={g}^{′}(2){f}^{-1}(2)+\\frac{g(2)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1+\\frac{(-1)}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{(-1)}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279608 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2a80aa-7882-50eb-bfe0-c0eeab72161d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Derivative Using Properties of Inverse Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de19756b-6c9e-563b-9cb1-5a0b90131f73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279610}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de19756b-6c9e-563b-9cb1-5a0b90131f73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279610} :answer-field/id #uuid "12d98025-b7f2-5a95-9b83-f1927db5e6a3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279609}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8b310eff-a1c1-5674-b489-414dfca2158d" :question/math-academy-id "q-342621" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=x-\\sqrt{\\frac{x+1}{3}}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain $x\\ge 1,$ determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1).$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(1).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(1)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(1) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =1 \\\\ x-\\sqrt{\\frac{x+1}{3}} & =1 \\\\ \\sqrt{\\frac{x+1}{3}} & =x-1 \\\\ \\frac{x+1}{3} & =(x-1)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{x+1}{3} & ={x}^{2}-2x+1 \\\\ x+1 & =3({x}^{2}-2x+1) \\\\ x+1 & =3{x}^{2}-6x+3 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-7x+2 & =0 \\\\ (3x-1)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3},2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the domain of $y=f(x)$ is $x\\ge 1,$ we have ${f}^{-1}(1)=2.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =x-\\sqrt{\\frac{x+1}{3}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =1-\\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{\\frac{x+1}{3}}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =1-\\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{\\frac{2+1}{3}}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =1-\\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{1}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =1-\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{5}{6})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279611 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b492068c-2bd2-5611-baab-780959d24c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function Using a Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6cf7dbb-640b-5e4e-a3c6-8fb004e30a86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279613} {:answer/id #uuid "6bd22d9a-a03c-58f5-9efb-0242510aa540" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279614} {:answer/id #uuid "6af6f94c-bc91-57b6-9252-af1c97252b71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279615} {:answer/id #uuid "88764d7d-1cd8-51c9-8f83-e7a18a77daf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279616} {:answer/id #uuid "3eb15566-a3e1-5697-ba26-726584dd31c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279617}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6cf7dbb-640b-5e4e-a3c6-8fb004e30a86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279613} :answer-field/id #uuid "18d441e8-f2ee-5ce8-87fd-cc518548578d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279612}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "49f0cdee-0fae-592b-8a52-db3806d6bdc5" :question/math-academy-id "q-4962" :question/problem "The following table gives the values of functions $g(x)$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points. Find $({g}^{-1})^{′}(-2).$\n\n| **$x$** | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ | $4$ | $3$ |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $6$ |\n| $1$ | $-2$ | $8$ |" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({g}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{g}^{′}({g}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n({g}^{-1})^{′}(-2)=\\frac{1}{{g}^{′}({g}^{-1}(-2))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${g}^{-1}(-2)=1$ and ${g}^{′}(1)=8.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({g}^{-1})^{′}(-2) & =\\frac{1}{{g}^{′}({g}^{-1}(-2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{g}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279618 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b492068c-2bd2-5611-baab-780959d24c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function Using a Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00215d8d-3c88-53a5-b3f6-f6544c1b3deb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279620} {:answer/id #uuid "988022e4-7957-5221-a489-a318fdb6b512" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279621} {:answer/id #uuid "b1ad5966-2d46-50b5-9119-6be2b62c4917" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279622} {:answer/id #uuid "11ab7dd8-1ed6-5d53-896b-c3a66f271c63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279623} {:answer/id #uuid "d47c6600-01dc-5d33-b6e8-011f79801de1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279624}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11ab7dd8-1ed6-5d53-896b-c3a66f271c63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279623} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0b3347d-fb13-5fdf-8c5e-5cbc7ca101dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279619}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6b67208f-6f32-509e-9ceb-61d4d53fe2b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-75834" :question/problem "The following table gives the values of functions $g(x)$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points. Find $({g}^{-1})^{′}(2).$\n\n| **$x$** | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $3$ | $-6$ |\n| $5$ | $2$ | $-5$ |\n| $7$ | $-2$ | $2$ |" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({g}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{g}^{′}({g}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n({g}^{-1})^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{{g}^{′}({g}^{-1}(2))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${g}^{-1}(2)=5$ and ${g}^{′}(5)=-5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({g}^{-1})^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{g}^{′}({g}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{g}^{′}(5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-5)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279625 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2a80aa-7882-50eb-bfe0-c0eeab72161d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Derivative Using Properties of Inverse Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "936ab591-12a6-5713-a1da-87be5d9c6d15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279627} {:answer/id #uuid "34cb3e00-3c88-50ba-af4e-c0ca88bab101" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279628} {:answer/id #uuid "b1eba020-968d-5a9c-9f3d-795ffaaa5127" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279629} {:answer/id #uuid "99cd4340-3a82-5bec-a978-d7988c291072" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279630} {:answer/id #uuid "0342f18f-0694-5143-a372-a4895c9aebbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279631}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1eba020-968d-5a9c-9f3d-795ffaaa5127" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279629} :answer-field/id #uuid "f55768e6-67b0-5a70-91a9-fee053091581" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279626}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e0131ab8-e5fa-5085-adfc-84de0cb74b5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-76106" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{2x-1}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(3).$" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(3),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(3)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(3))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(3).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(3)=x.$ For $x>\\frac{1}{2}$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(3) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =3 \\\\ \\sqrt{2x-1} & =3 \\\\ 2x-1 & =9 \\\\ 2x & =10 \\\\ x & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(5),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\sqrt{2x-1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-1}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(5) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\cdot 5-1}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(5) & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(3) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(3))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
